          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-0bd9dd71e718a28a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"The speaker directly avows a past loving state: “io, che t’amavo con tanta pena” (“I, who loved you with such anguish”). Its rhetorical and emotionally charged use does not independently enact a commitment or function as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal may be self-serving or accusatory, but those contextual purposes do not materially alter its dominant truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A directly spoken retrospective love avowal embedded in a rhetorical question addressed to Lora.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The man invokes his former anguished love and his claim that he fled to avoid harming Lora as a reproach after learning that she became Rafa’s mistress. The utterance therefore serves an argumentative, possibly manipulative appeal for recognition, but its core remains an avowal of a past emotional state. His murmured delivery after a pause supplies emotional framing, not independent expressive/reflexive force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Sì, Lora, io, che t’amavo con tanta pena” (“Yes, Lora, I, who loved you with such anguish”).\",\"supports\":\"The first-person affirmative wording explicitly presents past love for the addressee as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The speaker continues: “io, che fuggivo disperatamente per non farti male?” (“I, who fled desperately so as not to hurt you?”).\",\"supports\":\"The love avowal is used as part of a retrospective explanation and reproach rather than as a present relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He speaks “after being silent,” and the narration marks the utterance with “mormorò” (“murmured”).\",\"supports\":\"The delivery is controlled and rhetorically situated; although affectively intense, it gives no positive evidence of reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative Italian imperfect “t’amavo,” with past tense/aspect.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the core proposition concerns an ongoing or durative loving state in the past.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the retrospective avowal. Its reproachful purpose, emotional intensity, and potentially manipulative framing are adequately treated as context and create no residual pressure for P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
